tools agent_message send --to agent-01M2K1RFG71SJETW3996TFKAT0 --message-stdin <<'EOF'
Plan only, nothing changed or built. Read from the source (not run on his PC); each item: plan / risk / unsure.

1 AI chats: chats are never stored. The history lives in memory only and "New chat" already wipes it (closing the app also loses it). So there are no old chats to see. Plan: keep it that way (that is exactly "delete on New chat"), maybe add a confirm-free clear. Risk none. Unsure: whether he wants saved chat history instead; his wording says delete, so I'd leave it.
2/3 Missed cover title: not proven. Likely cause: Tesseract binarises the page, and huge coloured title text on an orange/white image (and very large glyphs) is a known weak spot; I run one pass on a 300 dpi RGB render with default layout. Plan: if a page yields few words, add a second pass on a grayscale + contrast-stretched copy (and an inverted one), and a half-size pass for huge text; merge non-overlapping words. Risk: slower on weak pages, may still miss stylised titles. Unsure: I haven't seen his page; I can test on a synthetic orange title. Honest limit: Tesseract is mediocre on designed covers.
4 Re-OCR: yes. The code OCRs every page in the range even if it already has text. Plan: default "skip pages that already have text" (checked with MuPDF text extraction, free), with a checkbox to force. Risk low.
5 Zoom lag: I already defer re-render by 140 ms. Remaining lag is the full-resolution render on the UI thread. Simple fix: render the new zoom level on a background thread and keep showing the stretched old image until ready, plus cap texture size. Risk: medium-low; unsure how much it helps without his PC.
6 Speed / RTX 3050: a GPU speedup is not realistic with Tesseract (its GPU path is gone/unreliable and CUDA would add hundreds of MB). Realistic options: (a) skip pages with text (item 4), (b) 200 dpi instead of 300 (about 2x), (c) run pages in parallel across CPU cores (about 3-6x on his machine), together likely 4-10x. (d) Bigger option: use Windows' built-in OCR (the one he says works) through the Windows API: no bundled engine or 15 MB data, probably faster and better on his cover page, can use the GPU where Windows does. Risk: it needs a WinRT binding crate and cross-compiling it here is unproven; I can't run it under Wine. I'd do a-c now and offer d as a separate try with Tesseract as fallback. His call.
7 Go to page: page box in the toolbar showing current/total, type a number + Enter to jump. Risk none.
8 Compress did nothing because it only touches raw 8-bit DeviceRGB/DeviceGray images larger than 1600 px and skips everything else (ICC-based colour, indexed, already-JPEG images under 1600 px, which is typical for scanned books), so most files hit zero images; the JPEG quality slider only applies to images it touches. The result also is not forced smaller. Plan: also handle JPEG (DCT) and ICC images (decode, optionally downscale to a target DPI, re-encode at his quality), only keep a re-encoded image if it is smaller, show "N images recompressed, size before/after", and add a DPI choice. Risk: medium (colour handling); I can verify on Linux with a JPEG-heavy test PDF.
9 "protection...wow": ambiguous (praise or complaint). No action; worth one line asking if anything is wrong.
10 Filename: show it in the window title ("name.pdf - LitePDF", with * when unsaved) and a label in the toolbar. Risk none.
11 Tabs: a tab strip, each tab holds its own document, scroll, zoom and textures. Cost: each open PDF keeps MuPDF's document in RAM (roughly the size of its structures, lazily loaded) and I'd drop rendered page images of inactive tabs, so inactive tabs cost little. Risk: medium, it is a refactor of the app state, and it must be tested (switching tabs while editing, closing a tab with unsaved changes). Estimated new memory for a 2nd/3rd PDF: tens of MB, not hundreds.
12 Noted: nothing is built until he approves. Suggested order if he agrees: 4, 7, 10, 8, speed a-c, 2/3, 5, then tabs (11); d (Windows OCR) only if he wants it.
EOF
